-- #1617 — two impls of ONE interface at two DIFFERENT function-typed heads.
--
-- `headTyconTy`'s `_ => None` arm (compiler/types/typecheck.mdk) is documented as
-- "a type VARIABLE head has no head tycon", but it really means "anything that is
-- not a TyCon and not a TyTuple" — TyFun included. So `Int -> Int` and
-- `Bool -> Bool` land in ONE `noneHeadTag` bucket and cannot be discriminated.
--
-- HAND-DERIVED CORRECT ANSWER, from the source, before running:
--   `sz f` — f : Int -> Int   -> the `impl Sz (Int -> Int)` body  -> 5
--   `sz g` — g : Bool -> Bool -> the `impl Sz (Bool -> Bool)` body -> 9
--   so (sz f, sz g) == (5, 9) and this program must print True.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE PROJECTION AND NOT THE RAW TUPLE. The raw form prints `(5, 5)` here
-- and `(9, 9)` in permutation.mdk — i.e. the wrong answer is DECLARATION-ORDER
-- dependent. A `stdout: (5, 5)` pin would therefore DRAIN on any change that moved
-- this file from one wrong answer to the other wrong answer, and a reader would
-- close a live S0 on the strength of it. Comparing against the hand-derived answer
-- collapses every wrong answer to `False` and ONLY the correct one to `True`.
-- The projection is proven able to report True on this binary: control.mdk's shape
-- with `println ((sz W, sz V) == (5, 9))` prints True (measured 2026-08-14).
interface Sz a where
  sz : a -> Int
impl Sz (Int -> Int) where
  sz _ = 5
impl Sz (Bool -> Bool) where
  sz _ = 9
f : Int -> Int
f x = x + 1
g : Bool -> Bool
g b = b
main = println ((sz f, sz g) == (5, 9))
